{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 36, :comments ["<b>And the sound of a driven leaf shall chase them.</b> For thus it is written: ״וְכָשְׁלוּ אִישׁ בְּאָחִיו״ — and each man shall stumble against his brother, for this speaks of the division of hearts found among Israel more than among all the other nations, as it says: ״וְאֶתְכֶם אֱזָרֶה בַגּוֹיִם״ — and I will scatter you among the nations. Just as scattered stalks, none joined to its fellow, so too Israel: even when they are in the land of their enemies they are divided, each man from his brother. For though the way of exiles is to console one another, Israel is not so — they are scattered and divided even in exile, and each one shoves his fellow with force of hand and seeks to dislodge him from his position, to roll over him and fall upon him, like the curse of Ahijah the Shilonite, of whom it is said (I Kings 14:15): ״וְהִכָּה ה׳ אֶת יִשְׂרָאֵל כַּאֲשֶׁר יָנוּד הַקָּנֶה בַּמַּיִם״ — and HaShem will strike Israel as the reed sways in the water. For every reed is pushed and swayed by the wind blowing through it, and in addition to the pushing of the wind, every reed pushes its fellow; so it turns out that every reed is displaced twice: once because of the wind, a second time because of the neighboring reed that pushes against it, and so from one to the other until all of them stumble and fall. So too every man of Israel is driven by the wind — that is, by the kingdoms — according to the prophecy of Daniel, who compared all four kingdoms to wind: not to the wind of HaShem, but as it says (Daniel 7:2): ״וַאֲרוּ אַרְבַּע רוּחֵי שְׁמַיָּא מְגִיחָן לְיַמָּא רַבָּא״. And perhaps ״לְיַמָּא רַבָּה״ alludes to the Sea of Reeds, in which are קָנֶה וָסוּף, reed and rush, and his intention is toward Israel by the parable of the reeds: that beyond the shoving of the nations against Israel, each one pushes his fellow in his distress. Therefore He compared them here to a driven leaf, for the leaf is very weak and is driven by the wind blowing through it, and even so every leaf pushes its fellow and strikes it. So too every man of Israel in exile resembles a leaf driven by the aforementioned wind, and even so he chases after his fellow to strike him with the whip of his tongue — whether against the nations, or through evil speech in the Jewish street — and this is קוֹל עָלֶה, 'the sound of a leaf.' Therefore it says immediately afterward: ״וְכָשְׁלוּ אִישׁ בְּאָחִיו״ — and each man shall stumble against his brother." "And He further alluded in עָלֶה נִדָּף, 'a driven leaf,' to the abundance of idle talk — שִׂיחַת חֻלִּין — spoken without truth in all the streets of the city, in the manner of what is said (Psalms 1:3): ״וְעָלֵהוּ לֹא יִבּוֹל״ — and his leaf shall not wither, and the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded (Sukkah 21a): these are the idle conversations of the disciples of the Sages, etc. So too every man of Israel is driven and pursued by קוֹל עָלֶה, 'the sound of a leaf' — that is, the idle talk of evil speech, those who make their voice heard outside with a sound of echoing — קוֹל עֲנוֹת — ״הַקּוֹל קוֹל עָקֹב מִלֵּב עַל שִׁכְבֵי וְעֵרֵי״ (Sanhedrin 29a). For the masters of the tongue have made all Israel hear: there is none clean, for everyone rejoices in his fellow's trouble, and it would be like honey in his mouth to sweeten it if he found a place to speak against his fellow. And how many insults does this cup pass over them, and no one says, 'Restore!' — הָשֵׁב. And in our generation this trait alone would suffice to prolong our exile."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {0 "The Aramaic phrase from Daniel 7:2 is retained in Hebrew script as quoted; 'Sea of Reeds' puns on קנה וסוף (reed and rush) which the Kli Yakar reads as the literal flora of ים סוף.", 1 "The wordplay on עלה/שיחת חולין depends on the Sages' reading of ועלהו (Psalms 1:3) as שיחת חולין; 'idle talk' literally means 'chatter of profane things.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:39:00.033658773Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}